# Triangle – Minimum Path Sum (Logic of All 4 Approaches)

1. Recursion (Brute Force)

Logic:

- Start from the top element (0,0).
- At every position, we have two choices: move down (i+1, j) or diagonally (i+1, j+1).
- Calculate the minimum path sum for both choices.
- Add the current element to the minimum of both paths.
- Base case: When we reach the last row, return the current element.
- Finally, return the minimum path sum.

Time Complexity: O(2^n)

Space Complexity: O(n) recursion stack.

2. Memoization (Top-Down DP)

Logic:

- Start from the top element (0,0).
- Before calculating a state, check whether its answer is already stored in the DP array.
- If already calculated, return the stored answer.
- Otherwise, calculate both down and diagonal paths.
- Add the current element to the minimum of both paths.
- Store the result in dp\[i\]\[j\].
- Finally, return dp\[0\]\[0\].

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity: O(n²) DP array + O(n) recursion stack.

3. Tabulation (Bottom-Up DP)

Logic:

- Create a 2D DP array.
- Copy the last row of the triangle into the DP array because the minimum path sum from the last row is the element itself.
- Start traversing from the second-last row towards the first row.
- For every cell, calculate the down and diagonal costs using the values from the row below.
- Store the minimum of both choices in dp\[i\]\[j\].
- Continue until reaching the first row.
- Finally, return dp\[0\]\[0\].

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity: O(n²)

4. Space Optimized Tabulation

Logic:

- Instead of maintaining a 2D DP array, use two 1D arrays: prev and curr.
- Copy the last row of the triangle into the prev array.
- Start traversing from the second-last row towards the first row.
- For every cell, calculate down and diagonal using prev\[j\] and prev\[j+1\].
- Store the minimum result in curr\[j\].
- After completing one row, update prev = curr.
- Repeat until reaching the first row.
- Finally, return prev\[0\].

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity: O(n)

Important concepts to remember:

- Recursion: Explore all possible paths.
- Memoization: Recursion + storing previously calculated answers.
- Tabulation: Bottom-up iterative DP.
- Space Optimization: Store only the previous and current rows instead of the entire DP table.

